 "summary": "Of 350 synthetic movers hidden in NEOWISE-like skies with the real NEOWISE cadence, the search found 99% of those with W1−W2 = 0 moving at least 1″/yr with W2 ≤ 13 (100% at W2 = 14.5, 96% at W2 = 15.5, 100% of bright ones moving only 0.2″/yr), measured their motion to 0.1% (median), and reported 0.00 false movers per survey tile on mover-free skies. For redder objects (W1−W2 = 2, like cold brown dwarfs) it found 97% at W2 ≤ 14.5 and 0% at W2 = 15.5. On real NEOWISE data it recovered 8 of 9 known fast-moving stars (Kapteyn's Star, WISE 0855-0714, Barnard's Star, Luhman 16, Wolf 359, Lalande 21185, Proxima Centauri, Epsilon Indi B).",
 "method": "Injection-recovery: we build synthetic NEOWISE single-exposure detection tables that copy the real NEOWISE observing pattern (exposure times, scan ids and frames of real data, about 21 visits from 2014 to 2024), fill them with field stars, galaxies, passing asteroids and spurious detections, hide movers of known speed, direction and brightness in them, and run the survey's mover search without telling it where they are. Noise, detection limits and positional errors follow a model measured on real NEOWISE detections. A hidden mover counts as recovered when the search reports a mover within 5″ of it with a motion within 30% (or 0.1″/yr). False positives are counted on skies with no movers at all, and the search is also run on real NEOWISE data around stars whose motion Gaia DR3 measured.",
 "limits": [
  "Synthetic skies use high-Galactic-latitude source counts; crowded fields near the Galactic plane are not simulated at their full density.",
  "Every exposure covers the whole synthetic patch; real frame edges and gaps are only present when injecting into real tables.",
  "The noise model is calibrated on two real 4′ NEOWISE fields; it has no blending, no position-dependent systematics and a simple saturation model.",
  "Injected movers have parallaxes from a tangential velocity of 80 km/s and do not accelerate (no binaries).",
  "False-positive rates are measured on synthetic skies; real artefacts (latents, ghosts, scattered light) are more varied.",
  "Known-star checks use small 4′ cut-outs of real data and, when available, the survey's own tile results."
 ],
